**W2-D7-Revision-Virtual_Environment**

---

```
KEY POINTS:
- venv = isolated Python environment per project
- without venv = all projects share packages = version conflicts
- python -m venv venv = creates venv folder
- venv\Scripts\activate (Windows) = activate venv
- source venv/bin/activate (Mac/Linux) = activate venv
- (venv) in terminal = confirmation venv is active
- always activate venv BEFORE installing anything
- pip freeze > requirements.txt = save installed packages
- pip install -r requirements.txt = restore packages
- never push venv folder to GitHub — too large
- always push requirements.txt to GitHub
- .gitignore = tells Git which files/folders to ignore
- deactivate = exit venv
```

---

```bash
# QUICK REFERENCE — ALL COMMANDS

# Create venv
python -m venv venv

# Activate (Windows)
venv\Scripts\activate

# Activate (Mac/Linux)
source venv/bin/activate

# Install packages (after activation)
pip install django
pip install djangorestframework
pip install psycopg2-binary
pip install python-decouple

# Save packages list
pip freeze > requirements.txt

# Install from requirements.txt (on new machine)
pip install -r requirements.txt

# Confirm Django installed
django-admin --version

# Deactivate
deactivate

# .gitignore contents
# venv/
# __pycache__/
# *.pyc
# .env
```

---

```
PROJECT STRUCTURE:
backend-dev/
├── venv/             ← never push to GitHub
├── requirements.txt  ← always push to GitHub
├── .gitignore        ← always push to GitHub
├── .env              ← never push (contains secrets)
└── manage.py         ← Django command tool

ANYONE CLONING YOUR PROJECT:
git clone your-repo
python -m venv venv
venv\Scripts\activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
python manage.py runserver
Done — exact same environment
```

